# modules/network/checks-httpport.rules - NET-09's own script-check registry
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; data/scoursh-network-scan-design/report.md §3.2
# item 3, §7 Tier 2).
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNER, NEVER A SHARED modules/network/checks.rules - the
# tension-29 discipline docs/FOUNDATION.md and modules/dast/passive/'s own
# split (AGENTS.md) both record: NET-06 (reachability.sh), NET-07 (banner.sh)
# and NET-08 (tlsport.sh) are peer tickets with no ordering against this one,
# and a shared registry is exactly the scheduling hazard tension 29 exists to
# remove. `checks-httpport.rules` is therefore this ticket's own file and no
# other ticket's records belong in it.
#
# THREE IDS, NOT ONE - the fingerprint, not a stylistic choice. The `net`
# location profile is (target, host, port, transport) (lib/findings.sh
# `_fp_components_for net`) and carries no "kind" or "product" component at
# all, so a name-only disclosure and a known-vulnerable-version hit on the
# SAME listener would collide onto the SAME fingerprint under one id -
# `modules/dast/passive/checks-banner.rules`'s own header states the
# identical reasoning for DAST-BANNER-*, and it applies here unchanged.
#
# "_HTTP_" IN EACH NAME IS DELIBERATE, NOT DECORATION: a future NET-07
# (banner.sh) reads a service's banner on a bare TCP connect - zero bytes
# sent - while this ticket sends one HTTP GET. Two different mechanisms
# reusing the SAME NET-SVC-BANNER_DISCLOSURE-01/NET-SVC-OUTDATED_COMPONENT-01
# spelling report.md §5.1's own table uses illustratively would need two
# DIFFERENT type tags (`passive` vs `safe-active`) on one check id, which
# §9.5's schema forbids (exactly one type tag per record) - so distinct ids
# were required regardless, and naming the channel in the id is what keeps a
# reader from assuming the two mechanisms are one check with two owners.
#
# Severity/confidence/cwe/owasp mirror
# modules/dast/passive/checks-banner.rules' own three records exactly - the
# underlying disclosure classes (a name in a response, a name+version, a
# version matched against the vendored list) are identical regardless of
# which channel read them, and report.md §3.4's backport caveat is the
# reason the outdated-component id stays confidence: medium rather than high
# here too.

id: NET-SVC-HTTP_SERVER_DISCLOSURE-01
title: Server or framework disclosed by an HTTP response on a non-standard port
script: httpport.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Suppress or overwrite the product token in the response -
  `server_tokens off` (nginx), `ServerTokens Prod` with `ServerSignature Off`
  (Apache), `expose_php = Off` (PHP), or removing `X-Powered-By` in the
  application framework - on this listener as well as the primary one, and
  strip it at the reverse proxy or CDN where the origin cannot be changed.
  Treat this as defence in depth and patch the component on its own schedule
  regardless.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: NET-SVC-HTTP_VERSION_DISCLOSURE-01
title: Framework or component version disclosed by an HTTP response on a non-standard port
script: httpport.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Stop publishing the exact version to unauthenticated clients on
  this listener - suppress the product token in the response header, remove
  the generator meta tag the CMS or static-site generator emits, and serve
  bundles under a content-hashed filename rather than a version-numbered one.
  Keep the component patched as well, because a version an attacker can
  fingerprint by behaviour is only hidden, not fixed.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: NET-SVC-HTTP_OUTDATED_COMPONENT-01
title: Component version on a non-standard HTTP listener named in the vendored known-vulnerable list
script: httpport.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-1104
owasp: A06:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Upgrade the component on this listener to a release the advisory
  does not name, or apply the vendor backport for it. Where an upgrade
  cannot be immediate, put a compensating control in front of the specific
  weakness the advisory describes and track the upgrade as the remediation
  rather than treating the control as one. Re-read the running version from
  the same listener afterwards. The version was read from an HTTP response
  banner, so a distribution that backports fixes under an unchanged upstream
  version string would not be visible here (report.md §3.4) - confirm before
  treating this as a high-confidence claim about the running binary.
references: CWE-1104
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A06_2021-Vulnerable_and_Outdated_Components/
